Modulon Language
================

Modulon is a C-derived programming language that aims to
preserve the power and familiarity of C while simplifying common
programming tasks.

OVERVIEW
--------

Modulon combines C-style syntax with convenient language features,
including inferred variable types, automatic string sizing, pattern
matching, and optional just-in-time compilation.

FEATURES
--------

* C-derived syntax with support for familiar C types.
* Variable declarations using the "var" keyword and type inference.
* "include" and "define" directives instead of C preprocessor syntax.
* Automatic string sizing and byte counting.
* Automatic memory management at compile time, without a garbage
  collector, alongside options for manual memory management.
* Pattern matching with ranges and a wildcard case.
* Logical AND (&&) and OR (||) operators.
* "typeof" for examining types.
* "input" for prompted user input.
* Formatted output, including automatic type formatting with %v.
* Ahead-of-time (AOT) and just-in-time (JIT) compilation.
* Built-in assembler support.
* Consistent language types across supported target environments.

SOURCE FILES
------------

Modulon source files use the .mlon extension.

EXAMPLE
-------

The following illustrates the intended syntax:

    var message = "Hello from Modulon!";
    printf("%v\n", message);

The exact commands needed to build and run this example depend on the
compiler version and local build configuration.

BUILDING
--------

Obtain the Modulon source code from the project's official repository.
Consult the build scripts and documentation included with that revision
for compiler requirements, build commands, and supported platforms.

COMPILATION
-----------

Modulon supports ahead-of-time compilation and a JIT mode. The JIT mode
can be selected with the --jit option in compiler versions that provide
it. Check your compiler's help output for the complete command syntax.

PROJECT GOALS
-------------

* Keep low-level programming practical and accessible.
* Reduce repetitive declarations and manual bookkeeping.
* Preserve explicit control when programmers need it.
* Provide useful diagnostics and predictable compilation behavior.
* Develop an approachable, capable alternative within the C family.

CONTRIBUTING
------------

Bug reports, reproducible test cases, documentation improvements, and
source code contributions are welcome. Before submitting changes,
review the contribution instructions in the project repository, if
available. Include a clear description of the problem and your fix.

